+# Somnia Documents: WordCraft worker spike
+
+Pinned upstream: `7584b9b2930ffddfe7db96b6eba977262e55135c`.
+Date: 2026-10-08. No Somnia changes, no remote push or merge.
+
+## Result
+
+GO for a bounded document-engine integration prototype. NOT a claim of Word-level
+DOCX fidelity or an editor ready to ship. A dedicated module Worker loads headless
+Rust/WASM, reads an actual OOXML ZIP, extracts body text (including table cells),
+paginates through WordCraft layout, rasterises page one with WordCraft's CPU
+renderer, inserts text through the Rust document model, exports DOCX and reopens
+it. The sample's heading, table, Unicode and inserted text were visually inspected
+in `results/after.png` and `results/page-one.png`.
+
+All parsing, layout, edits, raster and serialization run in the Worker. The main
+thread handles file input, presentation and RPC only. No egui, Window, DOM,
+OffscreenCanvas, GPU, SharedArrayBuffer, COOP/COEP or native UI dependencies.
+This is single-threaded WASM *inside* a dedicated Worker, not WASM thread support.
+
+## Run the prebuilt demo
+
+```
+python3 -m http.server 8765 --directory web
+# Open http://localhost:8765
+```
+
+Use Open fixture, open your own DOCX, insert a prefix, export DOCX. Do not open
+index.html via file://: module Workers/WASM need an HTTP origin. This demo is
+local-only, has no upload server, telemetry or external runtime requests. Serve
+with application/wasm MIME for the WASM file. Modern Chrome tested; other browsers
+and Windows Tauri WebView2 not tested.
+
+## Reproduce from upstream + patch
+
+```
+git clone https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft.git
+cd wordcraft
+git checkout 7584b9b2930ffddfe7db96b6eba977262e55135c
+git apply /path/to/wordcraft-worker.patch
+rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown
+# Install wasm-bindgen-cli EXACTLY 0.2.129 (prebuilt upstream release or cargo install)
+spikes/somnia-documents/scripts/build.sh
+cargo test --locked -p wordcraft-somnia-worker --lib
+cd spikes/somnia-documents
+npm ci
+CHROME_PATH=/usr/bin/google-chrome npm test
+```
+
+Build used Rust 1.99.0, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129, Playwright 1.58.2, Linux x86_64,
+headless Chrome 154.0.0.0. Rust sources retain upstream's >=1.90 requirement, but
+older compiler compatibility was not independently tested. Lockfile is included.
+Prebuilt WASM and JS are in the bundle, not the patch. Source fixture generation
+uses Python's standard library only, creates WordprocessingML ZIPs independently
+of WordCraft, and releases its original fixture text as CC0. These are real DOCX
+packages but synthetic fixtures, NOT Word-produced or customer documents.
+
+## Measurements
+
+Local HTTP, five sequential warmed 1-page samples, performance.now measured in
+Worker (not upstream DocLayout.ms, which is always zero on wasm). These are smoke
+measurements on this machine, not product performance guarantees.
+
+| Measurement | Result |
+|---|---:|
+| Release WASM | 10,033,084 bytes (9.57 MiB) |
+| gzip WASM | 4,075,188 bytes (3.89 MiB) |
+| JS wrapper | 10,323 bytes |
+| Cold fetch + compile + init | 104.4 ms |
+| Cold parse / layout | 8.4 / 28.6 ms |
+| Warm median parse / layout | 0.3 / 1.4 ms |
+| Warm median render page 1 PNG | 17.9 ms |
+| Warm median insert / incremental layout | 0.1 / 0.5 ms |
+| Warm median DOCX export | 1.2 ms |
+| 500-paragraph fixture | 34 pages |
+| Large parse / layout / page 1 render | 2.6 / 55.6 / 27.5 ms |
+| Main-thread timer ticks during large open | 11 in 59.5 ms wall time |
+
+Raw samples and browser identification are in results/metrics.json. Timer ticks
+show main-thread activity, not a guarantee of input latency. PNG rendering includes
+PNG encoding. Transfer/RPC and browser image decoding are not counted in Worker
+stage timings. Heap peak, long sessions and low-end hardware were not measured.
+
+## Tests
+
+Rust adapter tests: parse-edit-export-reopen equality, malformed ZIP rejection,
+invalid paragraph/path/offset does not mutate content, UTF-8 boundary validation.
+Browser tests: 5 export/reopen text equalities, malformed input rejected, invalid
+edit rejected, later session remains usable, 34-page pagination, main-thread ticks,
+actual raster PNG and screenshot, no page errors. Browser test found and fixed an
+adapter lifecycle bug: null the released session before opening the next file, so
+a failed open cannot leave a freed pointer. No upstream/vendor source patch needed.
+
+## Integration estimate (planning judgment, one experienced developer)
+
+- 2-4 working days: pin/vendor the headless crates and notices, typed Worker RPC,
+  open/save bridge for web and Tauri, cancellation by terminating a Worker,
+  viewport page virtualisation and error states. This is a viewing/export milestone.
+- Another 2-4 weeks: real editable page interaction (hit-test mapping, caret,
+  selection, keyboard, IME, clipboard, undo, styles/tables), model command adapter,
+  accessibility and Windows WebView2 verification. This spike only inserts text;
+  it does not wrap all 389 upstream commands or the engine session layer.
+- 2-6+ additional weeks for a representative external DOCX corpus, unsupported-part
+  detection/preservation, fidelity fixes, resource budgets and regression gates.
+  Complex Word workflows may exceed this. Do not use upstream roadmap hour estimates
+  as a Somnia delivery promise.
+
+Recommended host boundary: transfer file ArrayBuffers to a worker; keep the Rust
+Document and layout cache there; render only visible pages; return raster buffers
+or display lists and selection geometry. Persist approved exports through Somnia's
+existing file bridge, with original files preserved and explicit format-loss warnings.
+
+## Concrete limits / release gates
+
+- Only body plain text, top-level paragraph byte-offset insertion, paginate,
+  page-index raster and save exposed. No selection, IME, undo, comments UI,
+  command registry, table editing UI or accessible document tree in this adapter.
+- UTF-8 byte offsets are NOT JavaScript UTF-16 caret offsets. Host needs conversion.
+  Inserted text uses default CharProps, not surrounding character-style inheritance.
+- Upstream roadmap says charts, SmartArt and OLE are dropped; real-world DOCX corpus
+  fidelity was not started. Unknown XML is skipped by reader. Saving can silently
+  lose unsupported content. Keep the original, warn or block save until detection
+  and preservation have been validated. This is not a lossless OOXML package editor.
+- Synthetic fixture only; no Word/LibreOffice open validation, imported images,
+  footnotes, revisions, pagination parity or arbitrary documents tested here.
+- Bundled fonts are used; system fonts unavailable in WASM. No optional Japanese
+  craft-fonts build input. Fonts, glyph coverage and layout differ from Word.
+- Input capped at 16 MiB; edit text capped at 1 MiB; scale 0.1..2.0; raster side
+  capped at 2400 px. Upstream adds ZIP/XML limits. These are guardrails, not a full
+  memory budget: images, decompressed data and pagination can still allocate heavily.
+- RPC has no cancellation/backpressure/deadline in demo. Worker termination is
+  required for stalled parsing. Production needs a bounded operation queue,
+  timeouts, explicit disposal, raster LRU, and untrusted-file fuzz tests.
+- PNG per-page roundtrip is convenient, not ideal for active editing. Consider
+  raw RGBA/ImageBitmap or a draw-list renderer after interaction requirements settle.
+- No browser portability, Tauri filesystem integration, CSP or sustained-memory
+  validation. No Somnia integration performed or implied.
+
+## License and provenance
+
+WordCraft code is MIT OR Apache-2.0; choose MIT for Somnia vendoring, keep copyright,
+LICENSE-MIT, NOTICE and attribution. Copies of both upstream licenses and NOTICE
+are included. Embedded fonts are OFL-1.1; preserve the four OFL texts. Public-domain
+Moby dictionary / CC0 hyphenation retain attribution. Transitive dependencies have
+permissive SPDX declarations, including Unicode-3.0 obligations (not merely MIT
+and Apache). `notices/dependency-licenses.json` inventories this adapter's resolved
+non-dev WASM dependency closure and dependency license/notice files are bundled.
+Metadata audit is not legal advice or a complete SBOM/security audit.
+
+Do NOT copy docs/brand ArtCraft trademarks/logos into Somnia. They are excluded
+from the open-source grant; the upstream README says modified forks must remove
+them. No brand assets or upstream UI/icons were used by this demo. No CRAFT_FONTS_DIR
+optional fonts included. Any broader vendoring requires re-auditing new assets
+and dependencies. All adapter changes are new files and Cargo.lock updates;
+existing upstream engine source is unchanged.
